.TH DU 1 2026-10-20 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
du \- report the disk space used by files and directories
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B du
.RB [ \-abchHklLmsx ]
.RB [ \-d
.IR N ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B du
prints the disk space used by each
.I file
and, for a directory, by everything below it.
With no
.IR file ,
it reports on the current directory.
This
.B du
is the busybox applet; see
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
Each line of output is a size, a tab, and a name.
The name of a
.I file
is as given.
The name of an entry below a directory is the directory's name, a
.BR / ,
and the entry's name, with no second
.B /
when the directory's name already ends in one, and none after a bare
drive such as
.BR c: .
A directory's line comes after the lines of the entries below it, which
appear in the order Windows lists them.
.PP
There is a line for each
.IR file ,
and for each directory below one.
.B \-a
adds a line for each other file, and
.B \-d
and
.B \-s
remove lines.
A directory's size includes everything below it, whether or not that
has a line of its own.
.PP
The size of a file is an estimate of the space allocated to it, described
under
.BR NOTES ,
not the length of its data.
A size counts units of 1024 bytes, or of 512 bytes when
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-h ,
.BR \-k ,
and
.B \-m
change the unit or the size.
.SS What is counted
.IP \(bu 2
A file with several hard links is counted once, under the first of its
names that
.B du
reaches.
Its other names add nothing and get no line, even with
.BR \-a .
.IP \(bu 2
A directory that
.B du
has already reached is skipped: it is not counted and gets no line.
So
.B "du a a/b"
prints the same as
.BR "du a" ,
and
.B "du a/b a"
prints the lines for
.B a/b
and then those for
.BR a ,
whose size leaves out
.BR a/b .
.IP \(bu 2
A symbolic link counts as itself, and
.B du
does not enter a linked directory, unless
.B \-H
or
.B \-L
says to follow it.
.PP
.B \-l
turns off the first two rules.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-sh ,
and may follow the operands unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
The argument of
.B \-d
may be attached, as in
.BR \-d1 ,
or be the next argument.
There are no long options.
Of
.B \-H
and
.BR \-L ,
of
.BR \-h ,
.BR \-k ,
and
.BR \-m ,
and of
.B \-d
and
.BR \-s ,
the one given last applies.
.TP
.B \-a
Print a line for each file, not only for each directory.
Ignored with
.BR \-s .
.TP
.B \-b
Print apparent sizes: the length of each file in bytes, not the space
allocated.
Without
.BR \-h ,
.BR \-k ,
or
.BR \-m ,
sizes are in bytes, and with
.B \-k
or
.B \-m
they are rounded to the nearest unit.
A symbolic link counts the length of its target's name.
.TP
.B \-L
Follow all symbolic links.
A link counts as what it points to, and a link to a directory is
entered.
A link whose target cannot be examined is an error.
A directory reached again through a link is skipped, so a link that
points to its own directory does not loop.
With
.B \-l
it does, until the path is too long to examine, which is an error.
.TP
.B \-H
Follow symbolic links named as operands.
Once one is followed, links below it are followed too, as with
.BR \-L .
This applies to each operand separately.
.TP
.BI "\-d " N
Print a line only for an entry at most
.I N
levels below an operand.
.I N
is a decimal integer from 0 to 2147483647.
.B \-d 0
prints only the operands, and
.B \-d 1
adds the entries directly in them.
Sizes still include everything below.
.TP
.B \-c
After all operands, print a line named
.B total
with the sum of the operands' sizes.
Anything skipped as already counted is not in the sum.
.TP
.B \-l
Count and print a file once for each of its hard links, and do not skip
a directory that has been reached before.
.TP
.B \-s
Print only a line for each operand.
This is the same as
.BR "\-d 0" .
.TP
.B \-x
Skip any entry on a different volume from the operand it is below: it
is not counted and gets no line.
Volumes are told apart by serial number, and a link is on the volume
that holds the link, not the one that holds its target.
So with
.BR \-L ,
a link to a directory on another volume gets a line, but nothing in
that directory is counted, even when the link is an operand.
.TP
.B \-h
Print sizes as a number with one decimal place and the suffix
.BR K ,
.BR M ,
.BR G ,
.BR T ,
.BR P ,
or
.B E
for the largest power of 1024 that fits, as in
.BR 1.4M .
A size under 1024 bytes, possible only with
.BR \-b ,
is a plain number of bytes, and a size of 0 is
.BR 0 .
.TP
.B \-m
Print sizes in units of 1048576 bytes.
Without
.BR \-b ,
a size that is not a whole number of units is rounded up, so a nonzero
size is at least 1.
.TP
.B \-k
Print sizes in units of 1024 bytes.
This is the default unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set, which
.B \-k
overrides.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
output to a console is not converted to its code page.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
When set, even to an empty value, the default unit is 512 bytes, and
the first operand ends the options.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.BR BB_GLOBBING ,
are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
A
.I file
or an entry below one could not be examined, a directory could not be
opened, an option or the argument of
.B \-d
was invalid, or standard output could not be written.
.TP
.B 141
Standard output is a pipe that was closed before all output was written.
A closed pipe found only when
.B du
flushes its output at exit gives status 1 instead.
.PP
After a failure to examine or open something,
.B du
reports it on standard error and carries on.
A file that cannot be examined is reported as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
du: \fIname\fR: \fIreason\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
A directory that cannot be opened is reported as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
du: can\(aqt open \(aq\fIname\fR\(aq: \fIreason\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
and gets no line.
An invalid option prints a message and the usage on standard error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Show the size of each directory directly under
.IR project :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ du \-hd 1 project
3.4M	project/build
4.0K	project/docs
60.0K	project/src
3.4M	project
.EE
.RE
.PP
Find the largest entries, in KiB:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ du \-a project | sort \-rn | head \-4
3512	project
3444	project/build
3324	project/build/obj/main.o
3324	project/build/obj
.EE
.RE
.PP
Total several directories:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ du \-sc project/src project/docs
60	project/src
4	project/docs
64	total
.EE
.RE
.PP
Add up the lengths of files in bytes:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ du \-bc project/src/*.c
48000	project/src/main.c
9500	project/src/util.c
57500	total
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
The size of a file is its length rounded up to a multiple of 4096
bytes, the cluster size that
.B du
assumes whatever the volume uses.
An empty file is 0.
A compressed or sparse NTFS file counts the space it occupies on disk
instead of its length, rounded the same way, which can be much less.
A directory counts the length Windows reports for it, which is 0 for a
small NTFS directory but not for one with many entries.
.B \-b
counts lengths instead.
.PP
.B du
treats a symbolic link, a junction, a volume mount point, and an app
execution alias alike, as a symbolic link.
One that is not followed counts as 4096 bytes, or with
.B \-b
the length of its target's name.
.PP
Paths may use
.B /
or
.BR \e ,
but
.B du
adds
.B /
after a name that ends in a backslash, so the entries of
.B dir\e
are named
.BR dir\e/sub .
A drive-relative path such as
.B c:dir
works, and its entries are named
.BR c:dir/sub .
A bare drive such as
.B c:
is the current directory of that drive, and its entries are named
.BR c:sub .
A full path of 260 characters or more is reported as not found.
.PP
When started from
.BR cmd.exe ,
.B du
expands wildcards in its operands; see
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
Differences from GNU
.BR du :
.IP \(bu 2
There are no long options, such as
.BR \-\-max\-depth ,
and no
.BR \-0 ,
.BR \-B ,
.BR \-D ,
.BR \-P ,
.BR \-S ,
.BR \-t ,
or
.BR \-X .
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-a
is ignored with
.BR \-s ,
where GNU
.B du
rejects the combination.
.IP \(bu 2
Lines end with LF, not CRLF.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR df (1),
.BR find (1),
.BR ls (1)
